# The provider label's CPU-node half, 2026-11-27
#
# Why this exists
#   Backlog row 55: the label says "all nodes on CUDA" or "nodes left on CPU",
#   read from a throw-away session built with session.disable_cpu_ep_fallback=1.
#   The only fixture in the tree was a one-node Conv, which the CUDA EP covers,
#   so only the first half had ever been produced - the half a real model trips
#   was documented and never observed.
#
# Machine: Windows 11, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060, MSVC 14.50, onnxruntime 1.20.1
# GPU package (CUDA 12 provider) at .validation/ort-gpu-1.20.1, contract harness
# built from testkit/, template built with OFXPLUGIN_BUNDLE_CUDA_PROVIDER=ON.
#
# Which operator lands on the CPU EP - the experiment that chose the fixture.
# Three candidates, each one node, each shaped like its input, measured through
# the contract harness with --label-param provider and no golden:
#
#   Hardmax                    -> "nodes left on CPU", 96 checks, 0 failed
#   RandomNormalLike           -> session creation fails: 30 checks, 1 failed
#   MeanVarianceNormalization  -> session creation fails: 30 checks, 1 failed
#
# The last two prove nothing about the label: a model that cannot load never
# reaches the question. Hardmax has no CUDA kernel in 1.20.1, is deterministic
# (one-hot along the width axis) and keeps the frame image-shaped, so it is the
# fixture: tools/make-testmodels.py writes it, and the identical bytes are
# committed as templates/onnx-inference-ofx/models/cpu_node.onnx.
#
# Hardmax, verbatim - two label reads per run, instance creation and render:
#   ok 31 - label parameter 'provider' = "CUDA (device 0, 1 thread, bundled CUDA, tf32 off, arena kSameAsRequested, nodes left on CPU) - fallback: This session contains graph nodes that are assigned to ..."
#   ok 79 - label parameter 'provider' = "CUDA (device 0, 1 thread, bundled CUDA, tf32 off, arena kSameAsRequested, nodes left on CPU) - fallback: This session contains graph nodes that are assigned to ..."
#   contract_test: 96 checks, 0 failed
#
# The same run inside the suite (test.bat, GPU runtime root) - the step both
# runners now carry, where the label is asserted and not merely printed:
#   cpu-node label: models\cpu_node.onnx - Hardmax, which the CUDA EP does not implement
#   ok 31 - label parameter 'provider' = "CUDA (device 0, 1 thread, bundled CUDA, tf32 off, arena kSameAsRequested, nodes left on CPU) - fallback: This session contains graph nodes that are assigned to ..."
#   ok 79 - label parameter 'provider' = "CUDA (device 0, 1 thread, bundled CUDA, tf32 off, arena kSameAsRequested, nodes left on CPU) - fallback: This session contains graph nodes that are assigned to ..."
#   all checks passed
# (148 x 3 host profiles, then this run's 96, then 150, then 84.)
#
# The control: the one-node Conv, same bundle and arguments, label reads
# "all nodes on CUDA" - what the suite produced before this change, so the new
# step tells the two answers apart instead of printing the same one.
#
# Without a CUDA provider in the runtime root (CPU package, the CI shape) the
# step prints the reason and moves on:
#   NOT VERIFIED: no CUDA provider beside the .ofx, so the CPU-node half of
#     the label cannot be produced here - point ONNXRUNTIME_ROOT at the CUDA
#     package to run this step.
#   all checks passed
#
# What this proves: on this runtime, with this fixture, the label admits a node
# the CUDA EP cannot run and carries the runtime's own reason, and the assertion
# lives in both runners instead of in a document. What it does not prove: which
# node a real model leaves behind - the reason is the runtime's sentence, not a
# node name, which needs the verbose log (onnx-and-tensorrt.md section 6) - and
# nothing about a provider other than 1.20.1's CUDA EP.
